1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,099
Sam opens Harlan's ad account most Monday mornings before anything else gets his

2
00:00:05,099 --> 00:00:05,692
attention.

3
00:00:05,692 --> 00:00:10,382
This particular Monday the summary line for the last batch of statics read like

4
00:00:10,382 --> 00:00:14,952
every other Monday: an average click-through rate, unremarkable, nothing that

5
00:00:14,952 --> 00:00:16,199
screamed "look here".

6
00:00:16,199 --> 00:00:19,582
What would you have done with a number that unremarkable?

7
00:00:19,582 --> 00:00:22,491
He nearly moved straight past it to the next tab.

8
00:00:22,491 --> 00:00:27,418
Most people would read a summary number like that the way Sam nearly did: picture a

9
00:00:27,418 --> 00:00:32,285
bell curve, a hump in the middle, most ads clustered somewhere close to average, a

10
00:00:32,285 --> 00:00:33,710
few better, a few worse.

11
00:00:33,710 --> 00:00:38,636
That picture is wrong for almost every creative test you'll ever run, and believing

12
00:00:38,636 --> 00:00:41,129
it nearly cost Sam a result worth scaling.

13
00:00:41,129 --> 00:00:42,910
It's not a small error either.

14
00:00:42,910 --> 00:00:47,778
It's the kind of wrong assumption that quietly shapes every decision downstream of

15
00:00:47,778 --> 00:00:52,645
it: how you split budget, how many variants you bother making, even how you read a

16
00:00:52,645 --> 00:00:53,951
dashboard at a glance.

17
00:00:53,951 --> 00:00:58,343
Get the shape wrong and every one of those decisions inherits the mistake.

18
00:00:58,893 --> 00:01:03,771
Here's the twelve variants from Section zero again, this time by what they actually

19
00:01:03,771 --> 00:01:04,123
drove.

20
00:01:04,123 --> 00:01:05,710
Look at where the dots sit.

21
00:01:05,710 --> 00:01:10,293
Ten of them are crammed against the low end, barely distinguishable from zero.

22
00:01:10,293 --> 00:01:12,056
One sits a little further out.

23
00:01:12,056 --> 00:01:16,639
And one is all the way across the chart on its own, doing more than all eleven

24
00:01:16,639 --> 00:01:17,579
others combined.

25
00:01:17,579 --> 00:01:18,990
That's not a bell curve.

26
00:01:18,990 --> 00:01:23,867
That's a pile at the bottom with a long thin tail running off to one side, and it's

27
00:01:23,867 --> 00:01:28,568
the shape almost every real creative test actually takes, whether the product is

28
00:01:28,568 --> 00:01:32,035
socks, software, or anything else sold through paid social.

29
00:01:32,035 --> 00:01:34,444
Notice something else about this picture.

30
00:01:34,444 --> 00:01:39,204
If you'd stopped the test after looking at the first six or seven variants, you'd

31
00:01:39,204 --> 00:01:41,025
have seen nothing but the pile.

32
00:01:41,025 --> 00:01:45,902
The interesting result was waiting at the far end, and nothing about the early data

33
00:01:45,902 --> 00:01:47,900
would have told you it was coming.

34
00:01:47,900 --> 00:01:52,542
That's a separate problem from the shape itself, and it's exactly what the next

35
00:01:52,542 --> 00:01:54,305
lecture's volume maths solves.

36
00:01:54,855 --> 00:01:59,287
Let's name it properly, because the name tells you what to do differently.

37
00:01:59,287 --> 00:02:04,198
A normal distribution is the bell curve: most things are close to typical, and the

38
00:02:04,198 --> 00:02:09,169
further you get from typical in either direction, the rarer it gets, symmetrically.

39
00:02:09,169 --> 00:02:12,343
A heavy-tailed distribution doesn't behave like that.

40
00:02:12,343 --> 00:02:16,954
Most outcomes sit near zero, and every so often, one sits far out on its own,

41
00:02:16,954 --> 00:02:19,410
disproportionate to everything around it.

42
00:02:19,410 --> 00:02:22,943
You'll have met this shape before, even if nobody named it.

43
00:02:22,943 --> 00:02:27,675
It's the shape of book sales, where a handful of titles outsell everything else

44
00:02:27,675 --> 00:02:28,214
combined.

45
00:02:28,214 --> 00:02:33,244
It's the shape of a venture portfolio, where one investment returns the fund and the

46
00:02:33,244 --> 00:02:34,322
rest mostly don't.

47
00:02:34,322 --> 00:02:39,293
Creative testing follows the same pattern, and once you know to expect it, a lot of

48
00:02:39,293 --> 00:02:42,228
otherwise confusing results stop being confusing.

49
00:02:42,228 --> 00:02:47,258
Rank your own last test by actual result rather than by launch date, and look at the

50
00:02:47,258 --> 00:02:50,732
gap between your best performer and your middle performer.

51
00:02:50,732 --> 00:02:53,846
If that gap is large, you already have a heavy tail.

52
00:02:53,846 --> 00:02:57,499
Most testing programmes do, whether anyone's named it or not.

53
00:02:58,049 --> 00:03:02,140
Here's why that average is actively misleading, not just imprecise.

54
00:03:02,140 --> 00:03:06,901
When ten variants barely move and one dominates, the average sits somewhere in

55
00:03:06,901 --> 00:03:10,686
between, at a number none of the twelve variants actually hit.

56
00:03:10,686 --> 00:03:15,814
You could report "1.1 percent average CTR" and it would be true, exactly the kind of

57
00:03:15,814 --> 00:03:20,942
line Sam nearly scrolled past that Monday, and it would also describe a variant that

58
00:03:20,942 --> 00:03:23,201
does not exist anywhere in your data.

59
00:03:23,201 --> 00:03:28,024
This is exactly how a dashboard can tell two different stories to two different

60
00:03:28,024 --> 00:03:28,451
people.

61
00:03:28,451 --> 00:03:33,396
Someone glancing at "1.1 percent average" concludes the batch was mediocre across

62
00:03:33,396 --> 00:03:34,800
the board and moves on.

63
00:03:34,800 --> 00:03:39,928
Someone who ranks the same twelve variants individually finds a result worth scaling

64
00:03:39,928 --> 00:03:40,661
immediately.

65
00:03:40,661 --> 00:03:41,271
Same data.

66
00:03:41,271 --> 00:03:46,399
Completely different decision, depending on whether you looked at the summary or the

67
00:03:46,399 --> 00:03:46,765
shape.

68
00:03:46,765 --> 00:03:51,222
If you make decisions off that average, treating every variant as roughly

69
00:03:51,222 --> 00:03:56,228
equivalent, you'll under-invest in the one that's actually working and over-invest

70
00:03:56,228 --> 00:03:58,731
in eleven that were never going anywhere.

71
00:03:59,281 --> 00:04:02,983
So here's what this actually changes about how you run a test.

72
00:04:02,983 --> 00:04:07,999
An even split, the same budget to all twenty variants, feels fair and feels careful.

73
00:04:07,999 --> 00:04:10,627
Against a heavy-tailed result, it's neither.

74
00:04:10,627 --> 00:04:15,583
It spends real money proving that most of your variants are exactly as unremarkable

75
00:04:15,583 --> 00:04:20,420
as the shape predicted, and it slows down how fast you find and feed the one that

76
00:04:20,420 --> 00:04:20,779
isn't.

77
00:04:20,779 --> 00:04:25,496
The better move, and you'll build the actual mechanics of it in Section 7, is a

78
00:04:25,496 --> 00:04:30,333
fast, cheap first pass across everything, followed by concentrating spend hard on

79
00:04:30,333 --> 00:04:32,304
whatever's already pulling ahead.

80
00:04:32,304 --> 00:04:35,111
You're not being unfair to the losing variants.

81
00:04:35,111 --> 00:04:38,873
You're respecting the shape of the data instead of fighting it.

82
00:04:38,873 --> 00:04:42,456
This also changes what a good test plan looks like on paper.

83
00:04:42,456 --> 00:04:47,233
If someone hands you a plan that spends identically across every variant for the

84
00:04:47,233 --> 00:04:50,518
full test window, that's a plan built for a bell curve.

85
00:04:50,518 --> 00:04:55,414
A plan built for a heavy tail front-loads a cheap screening pass and reserves most

86
00:04:55,414 --> 00:04:58,042
of the budget for whatever the screen finds.

87
00:04:58,592 --> 00:05:02,795
I want to be honest about the part of this that's genuinely uncomfortable.

88
00:05:02,795 --> 00:05:07,453
If you run a batch of twenty and eighteen of them underperform, that can feel like

89
00:05:07,453 --> 00:05:11,258
eighteen failures, especially to anyone paying for the media spend.

90
00:05:11,258 --> 00:05:11,769
It isn't.

91
00:05:11,769 --> 00:05:15,802
In a heavy-tailed world, that's what a normal, healthy test looks like.

92
00:05:15,802 --> 00:05:18,812
The failure mode isn't generating variants that lose.

93
00:05:18,812 --> 00:05:21,936
It's stopping before you've found the one that doesn't.

94
00:05:21,936 --> 00:05:26,537
You will have to explain this to someone eventually: a founder, a client, a boss,

95
00:05:26,537 --> 00:05:31,081
watching a spend report full of small losses and asking whether the programme is

96
00:05:31,081 --> 00:05:31,535
working.

97
00:05:31,535 --> 00:05:34,772
The honest answer is that you can't tell from the losses.

98
00:05:34,772 --> 00:05:39,487
You can only tell from whether a tail has shown up yet, and that means holding your

99
00:05:39,487 --> 00:05:42,383
nerve through a report that looks worse than it is.

100
00:05:42,933 --> 00:05:48,002
Next lecture turns this shape into an actual number: how many variants you need to

101
00:05:48,002 --> 00:05:51,154
generate before a tail this thin reliably shows up.

102
00:05:51,154 --> 00:05:55,976
It's not infinite, and it's smaller than most people assume once you know what

103
00:05:55,976 --> 00:05:57,706
you're actually looking for.

104
00:05:57,706 --> 00:06:00,735
Before that, do the five-minute version yourself.

105
00:06:00,735 --> 00:06:05,927
Open your last test's results and rank them by what they actually drove, not by when

106
00:06:05,927 --> 00:06:06,793
they launched.

107
00:06:06,793 --> 00:06:10,069
Keep that ranked list somewhere you'll find it again.

108
00:06:10,069 --> 00:06:15,137
It's the first evidence Section 2.6's brief draws on when it turns "generate more"

109
00:06:15,137 --> 00:06:18,661
into a specific set of angles worth trying, artifact B01.
